{# Who made this, how to reach it, and what it cost to draw.

   The navigation menu arrives keyed by URL and the repository links keyed by
   label, so navigation is flipped here rather than in the column partial: the
   partial's job is to draw `label => url`, and teaching it about two shapes is
   how the old footer ended up with three loops that could not be shared. #}
{% set navigation = {} %}
{% for url, item in config('site.main-menu')|filter(item => item.enabled and (item.footer ?? true)) %}
    {% set navigation = navigation|merge({(item.text): url}) %}
{% endfor %}

{% set git = git_info() %}

<div class="footer__bottom">
    {# The mark to the left of both lines, the way the reference has it: the
       name on the first and the years on the second, and the logo centred
       against the pair rather than against the name alone.

       Which means the two lines are their own block. Indenting the years by the
       tile's width instead -- the first attempt -- lines them up under the name
       but leaves them below the tile, so the logo still belongs to one line.

       Two links to "/" and only one of them announced. The name is the link; the
       tile repeats it so the logo is clickable with a mouse, and is hidden from
       assistive tech and taken out of the tab order so nobody has to hear the
       same destination twice or tab through it to reach the socials. #}
    <div class="footer__brand">
        <div class="footer__brand-head">
            <a class="footer__brand-icon" href="/" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
                <i class="fas fa-plane-departure"></i>
            </a>
            <div class="footer__brand-lines">
                <a class="footer__brand-name" href="/">{{ config('app.name') }}</a>
                <p class="footer__copyright">
                    <i class="far fa-copyright" aria-hidden="true"></i> {{ copyright_years() }}
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        {# Icons alone, the way the reference has them. Each one still needs a
           name for anyone who cannot see which brand it is. #}
        <ul class="footer__socials">
            {% for title, item in config('site.footer-social') %}
                <li>
                    <a class="footer__social" href="{{ item.url }}"
                       target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="{{ title }}">
                        <i class="fa-brands fa-{{ item.ico }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </a>
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
        </ul>
    </div>

    {% include 'partials/footer/column.html.twig' with {
        'id': 'nav',
        'title': 'Navigation',
        'links': navigation,
        'landmark': 'Pages of ' ~ config('app.name')
    } only %}

    {% include 'partials/footer/column.html.twig' with {
        'id': 'repo',
        'title': 'Repository',
        'links': config('site.footer-git'),
        'external': true
    } only %}

    {# A real form, posting to a real endpoint, writing to a real table.

       There was one here before and it was taken out, because it accepted an
       address and dropped it. The comment that recorded its removal said what
       it would take to bring it back -- somewhere to put an address -- and that
       is what `subscribers` is.

       novalidate: the browser's own bubble says "Please enter an email address"
       in a tooltip that vanishes and is not announced. The field is still
       type="email", so the right keyboard comes up on a phone; the message is
       ours to place and to keep on screen.

       The id stays `fare-alerts` whatever the heading is called. It is where
       AjaxController sends a scriptless browser back to after the form posts,
       so renaming it to match the words above would land that redirect on a
       page with no such anchor and no message in sight. #}
    {% set notice = subscribe_notice() %}
    {# aria-labelledby, so the form is a landmark with a name.

       It has a visible <h2> and had no accessible name, which means it was not
       exposed as a form landmark at all -- the heading was there for the eye
       and nothing else. Pointing at the heading it already has costs one
       attribute and an id. #}
    <form class="footer__subscribe js-subscribe" id="fare-alerts"
          action="/ajax/subscribe" method="post" novalidate
          aria-labelledby="subscribe-title">
        {# What this form does is keep an address. What it does not do is send
           anything to it -- there is no mailer in this project -- and the copy
           used to say otherwise three times over: a heading promising an inbox,
           a line promising a note when a fare dropped, and a consent line
           saying the address was stored "to send those alerts".

           The form is still worth having, for exactly the reason its own
           comment above gives: the version before it accepted an address and
           dropped it, and `subscribers` is somewhere to put one. Collecting is
           the thing it can honestly claim, so that is what it claims. #}
        <h2 class="footer__title" id="subscribe-title">The fare-alert list</h2>
        <p class="footer__subscribe-text">
            Leave your address and it goes on the list. Nothing is sent yet
            &mdash; there is no mail behind this demonstration.
        </p>

        {# Both carried in the form, because without scripting there is no
           fetch to put the token in a header and no way to know afterwards
           which page the visitor was on. The endpoint refuses a return_to that
           is not one of our own paths. #}
        <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token() }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="return_to" value="{{ current_page() }}">

        <div class="footer__subscribe-row">
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="subscribe-email">Email address</label>
            <input class="footer__subscribe-input js-subscribe-email"
                   id="subscribe-email" type="email" name="email" required
                   autocomplete="email" placeholder="you@example.com">
            <button class="footer__subscribe-submit" type="submit">Join the list</button>
        </div>

        {# aria-live, because with the script running the answer arrives after
           the button has been pressed and focus has not moved: without it a
           screen reader is never told whether anything happened. Rendered full
           only for a browser that posted the form and was sent back here. #}
        <p class="footer__subscribe-note js-subscribe-note" role="status" aria-live="polite"
           {% if notice %}data-tone="{{ notice.tone }}"{% endif %}>{{ notice ? notice.message : '' }}</p>
        <p class="footer__subscribe-consent">We store the address, and nothing else.</p>
    </form>
</div>

<div class="footer__legal">
    <p>
        Software made by
        <a href="{{ config('meta.author.website') }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ config('meta.author.name') }}</a>
        as <a href="https://www.flighthub.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FlightHub</a>
        PHP Coding <a href="/Trip_Builder_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Assessment</a>.
        Code licensed
        <a href="{{ config('app.license.url') }}" target="_blank" rel="license noopener">{{ config('app.license.type') }}</a>,
        documentation <a href="{{ config('app.documentation') }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.
    </p>

    {# What the page cost to build. It used to sit in a column of its own beside
       the navigation, in <code>, which made the render time the loudest thing in
       the footer. It is worth keeping on a project whose point is how it is
       built -- it is not worth a column. #}
    <p class="footer__stats">
        {{ execution_time }}s &middot;
        {{ database_requests }} quer{{ database_requests == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies' }} &middot;
        {{ flights_count }} flights &middot;
        <a href="{{ git_repo() }}/commit/{{ git.commit_hash }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ git.tag }}-{{ git.branch }}-{{ git.commit_hash }}</a>
        posted {{ git.commit_date }}
    </p>
</div>
